{
  "concept": "The sine of an angle is the height (y-coordinate) of the corresponding point on the unit circle, so the graph of y = sin(x) is a running record of that height as the angle sweeps around the circle.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Left panel: faint gray unit circle centered at origin with horizontal and vertical axes drawn as double-headed arrows",
    "Left panel: gray fan of radii at the special angles (multiples of π/6 and π/4) crossing the circle",
    "Left panel: highlighted dark-blue radius from center to the moving point on the circle at the current angle",
    "Left panel: moving point on the circle with a small vertical drop segment to the horizontal axis showing its height",
    "Right panel: Cartesian axes, horizontal axis labeled x with arrowheads, vertical axis labeled y with arrowheads",
    "Right panel: y-axis tick labels 1, 0, and -1 with short tick marks",
    "Right panel: x-axis tick marks labeled π/6, π/4, π/3, π/2 (extendable to π, 3π/2, 2π)",
    "Right panel: solid dark-blue curve y = sin(x) with arrowheads at both ends, passing through the origin and peaking at 1",
    "Right panel: curve label 'y = sin(x)' in blue near the peak",
    "Right panel: four dashed orange vertical segments dropping from the curve to the labeled x-axis ticks",
    "Dashed teal horizontal connector lines running from points on the sine curve leftward across the gap to the matching heights on the gray circle",
    "Moving dark-blue tracer dot on the sine curve at the current angle",
    "Readout label showing current angle x and value sin(x)",
    "Optional shaded quadrant tint on the circle indicating whether the height is positive or negative"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "angle",
      "type": "slider",
      "label": "Angle x (radians)",
      "range": [0, 6.283, 0.01],
      "default": 0.524,
      "teaches": "Dragging the angle moves the circle point and the graph point together, so the learner sees that the graph's height at x is literally the circle point's y-coordinate."
    },
    {
      "id": "quarterSweep",
      "type": "animation",
      "label": "Sweep 0 → π/2 and trace the curve",
      "loop": true,
      "teaches": "Watching the radius rotate while the curve is drawn in real time makes the sine graph read as a continuous recording of vertical height on the circle."
    },
    {
      "id": "domainRange",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Show",
      "options": ["First quadrant (0 to π/2)", "Full revolution (0 to 2π)"],
      "default": "First quadrant (0 to π/2)",
      "teaches": "Extending past π/2 shows the height shrinking and then going negative below the x-axis, so quadrant position on the circle explains the sign of sine."
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "One angle, two pictures",
      "narration": "At x = π/6 the radius points up and to the right; the teal connector carries the circle point's height straight across to the graph, where the curve sits at exactly that height above π/6.",
      "state": { "angle": 0.524, "domainRange": "First quadrant (0 to π/2)", "quarterSweep": "idle" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Climbing through the special angles",
      "narration": "Drag on to π/4 and then π/3: the point rises on the circle and the tracer rises on the graph in step, with the dashed orange drop lines landing on the matching tick marks.",
      "state": { "angle": 1.047, "domainRange": "First quadrant (0 to π/2)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "The peak at π/2",
      "narration": "At π/2 the radius points straight up, the circle point's height equals the full radius of 1, and the sine curve reaches its maximum value of 1.",
      "state": { "angle": 1.5708, "domainRange": "First quadrant (0 to π/2)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Watch the record being written",
      "narration": "Play the quarter-turn sweep: the radius rotates from 0 to π/2 while the blue curve is drawn from the origin to the peak, one height at a time.",
      "state": { "quarterSweep": "running", "domainRange": "First quadrant (0 to π/2)" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Past the peak: sign follows quadrant",
      "narration": "Open the view to a full revolution and drag into the third quadrant: the circle point drops below the horizontal axis and the curve dips below the x-axis by exactly the same amount.",
      "state": { "domainRange": "Full revolution (0 to 2π)", "angle": 3.665, "quarterSweep": "idle" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "The sine curve isn't a separate object from the circle — it is the circle point's height plotted against the angle, so every feature of the graph (the rise to 1, the fall, the negative dip) is just where the point sits vertically on the circle.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Two panels side by side on a white field. Left: a light-gray unit circle with gray double-arrow axes through its center and a gray fan of radii at the π/6 and π/4 family of angles; the active radius and its endpoint are dark navy blue, with a thin drop line to the horizontal axis. Right: black double-arrow Cartesian axes, y labeled at top, x labeled at right, y-axis ticked and labeled 1 and -1 with 0 at the origin. The sine curve is a thick dark navy arc entering from the left through the origin, peaking at 1 above π/2 and bending down toward the right arrowhead; label 'y = sin(x)' in blue near the top right of the peak. Dashed orange verticals drop from curve points to x-axis ticks labeled π/6, π/4, π/3, π/2 in stacked fraction form. Dashed teal horizontals run from those curve points leftward across the gap between panels to the same heights on the circle — these teal lines are the visual argument and should stay prominent. Keep both panels on a shared vertical scale so a height of 1 on the circle lines up with y = 1 on the graph."
}

